#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172040 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171846 :question/id #uuid "3602a14d-6bd7-5586-a6e8-51d069dc8641" :question/math-academy-id "e-8507" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x+y?$ \n\n![](images/3b/3bd19865d2a1bb6bbb40c57112f269b51a3740d01da2c80b81db4eb84c6766f1.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/54/543bb98ff948d1186e05d84db7992f9d5033ba4eaf47a9787b6b5664b88510a2.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/43/433f0cf3835465e7a0cca195f8b0f0b280984200d84f49533219f08967adc497.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048179 :topic/id #uuid "8633737d-b07f-5466-99f0-a422586920c4" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172037}] :topic/math-academy-id 3351 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049722}] :topic/title "Slope Fields for Autonomous Differential Equations"}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672688 :question-generator/id #uuid "6a4f297a-4489-580b-be66-b2db5196163a" :question-generator/script "generators/ac/acb47adbd904782692588a736e205ab6e0543e545a864cfb555022c6ef3a3c23/17592186172040.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7544c8-1881-562c-a26c-67a